From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Builder <builder@example.invalid>
Date: Mon, 25 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] usb: phy_meson_usb: release POR after the ready poll

NOT VERIFIED ON HARDWARE. This is a hypothesis test, not a diagnosis. It
stacks on 0009, which was booted on this board and did not fix the failure:
both PHYs still printed "wait for ready timeout", then "set pll failed,
exit, val:0x0f549540", then "phy init failed --> -110", and neither USB
controller came up. 0009 is left in place -- whether its U2P_R0 write lands
was never measured, because that needs a U-Boot register read over serial
that has not been run.

What this adds is the second half of the host bring-up that 0009 does not
reproduce: the POR release.

The tail of meson_u2phy_aml_init() runs

    priv->cali(phy);
    ret = meson_usb2phy_wait_ready(phy, 200);
    if (ret)
            dev_err(phy->dev, " wait for ready timeout.\n");
    ret = priv->set_pll(phy);
    if (ret)
            return ret;

The ready timeout is not fatal: its ret is overwritten on the next line and
never propagated. -110 is returned by set_pll(), on a phy that had already
failed to report ready. So the write is placed between the two, which is the
last point before the call that actually fails.

The register recipe is copied from the driver's own host-mode setter, the
MESON_USB_MODE_HOST arm of meson_u2phy_set_mode() in the same file:

    reg0.d32 = readl(&phy->u2p_aml_regs[0]->r0);
    reg0.b.host_device = 1;
    reg0.b.POR = 0;
    //reg0.b.IDPULLUP0 = 1;
    //reg0.b.DRVVBUS0 = 1;
    writel(reg0.d32, &phy->u2p_aml_regs[0]->r0);

The two commented-out lines are not transcribed. 0009 already sets those two
bits earlier in the same function and this write preserves them.

That arm is unreachable on a cold init. s7d binds meson_u2phy_set_mode as
priv->set_mode in socs/phy-meson-s7d-usb.c, and both call sites in this file
are guarded by

    if (phy->suspend_flag && phy->current_mode != 0)

with current_mode zero out of the kzalloc() in meson_aml_u2phy_parse(). So
without this patch the phy reaches set_pll() with POR asserted -- asserted by
0009, and before 0009 asserted by the bootloader, which hands over U2P_R0
reading 0x0a on usbphy0 and 0x0b on usbphy1, both with bit 3 set.

host_device is set as well, because that is what the modelled arm does. It is
not a second change: 0009 sets host_device earlier in this same function,
and no code between the two writes touches U2P_R0 -- every writel to ->r0 in
this directory is inside meson_u2phy_legacy_set_mode() or
meson_u2phy_set_mode(), neither of which runs here. Whether the hardware
clears the register across the phycfg reset pulse is unmeasured; if it does,
host_device is restored, which is the state the driver wants either way.

One thing this patch is not: a closer copy of 5.15. The 5.15 driver that
works on this board, amlogic_crg_drd_usb2_init_v0() in
drivers/usb/crgdrdphy/phy-aml-crg-drd-usb2.c, sets POR and never clears it --
its POR = 1 is the only POR write in the function, and it runs its own
set_usb_pll() with POR still asserted. The release there comes from a mode
layer that runs after init, if at all. So a positive result from this patch
would mean the 6.12 PLL needs a condition the 5.15 PLL did not, not that 5.15
was being copied more faithfully.

Deliberately not changed: the PLL retry loop in
socs/phy-meson-s7d-usb.c discards the value it reads -- pll_val is reassigned
from def_val inside the loop, clearing bit 31, which the loop then polls, and
that arithmetic produces the observed 0x0f549540 exactly. It is left alone
because the 5.15 set_usb_pll_v3() has the identical loop, same discarded read
and same reassignment, and USB works on 5.15. Identical code cannot explain a
difference in outcome, and one behavioural change per build is what keeps a
bench result readable.

What this cannot show: the 6.12 image has no /dev/mem and the driver exposes
no register view, so whether the write lands has to be read from the U-Boot
prompt over serial. docs/task/BUG-010.md carries the procedure.
---
--- a/common_drivers/drivers/usb/phy_meson_usb/phy-meson-aml-usb2-com.c
+++ b/common_drivers/drivers/usb/phy_meson_usb/phy-meson-aml-usb2-com.c
@@ -852,6 +852,43 @@
 	if (ret)
 		dev_err(phy->dev, " wait for ready timeout.\n");
 
+	/*
+	 * Release POR before the PLL is brought up. This mirrors the
+	 * MESON_USB_MODE_HOST arm of meson_u2phy_set_mode() in this file,
+	 * which is the driver's own recipe for putting U2P_R0 into host
+	 * state: read, host_device = 1, POR = 0, write back. IDPULLUP0 and
+	 * DRVVBUS0 are commented out at that site, so they are not
+	 * transcribed here; the block above sets them and this write leaves
+	 * them alone. host_device is already 1 from that block and no code
+	 * between the two writes touches U2P_R0 in software, so POR is the
+	 * only bit whose value this changes.
+	 *
+	 * That arm never runs on a cold init. s7d binds
+	 * meson_u2phy_set_mode as priv->set_mode
+	 * (socs/phy-meson-s7d-usb.c), and both of its call sites are
+	 * guarded by "phy->suspend_flag && phy->current_mode != 0";
+	 * current_mode is zero out of the kzalloc() in
+	 * meson_aml_u2phy_parse(), so the guard is false and the phy
+	 * reaches set_pll() with POR still asserted.
+	 *
+	 * Placed after the ready poll and before set_pll() because set_pll()
+	 * is the call that fails. The -ETIMEDOUT from
+	 * meson_usb2phy_wait_ready() above is logged and then discarded --
+	 * ret is overwritten below -- so the -110 that reaches the phy core
+	 * comes from the PLL, on a phy that had already failed to report
+	 * ready. Releasing POR here targets the fatal step, not the logged
+	 * one.
+	 *
+	 * NOT VERIFIED ON HARDWARE, and not a closer copy of 5.15: 5.15
+	 * amlogic_crg_drd_usb2_init_v0() runs its own set_usb_pll() with POR
+	 * still asserted and never clears it. If this helps, the 6.12 PLL
+	 * needs something the 5.15 one did not.
+	 */
+	reg0.d32 = readl(&phy->u2p_aml_regs[0]->r0);
+	reg0.b.host_device = 1;
+	reg0.b.POR = 0;
+	writel(reg0.d32, &phy->u2p_aml_regs[0]->r0);
+
 	ret = priv->set_pll(phy);
 	if (ret)
 		return ret;
-- 
2.43.0
